by photography, film, and theatrical scenery; by writing, speech, and presumably even music and other ‘sound images.’” Mitchell describes landscape as a medium, i.e., “a material ‘means’ (to borrow Aristotle’s terminology) like language or paint, embedded in a tradition of cultural signification, a body of symbolic forms capable of being invoked and reshaped to express meanings and values” (W. J. T. Mitchell, “Imperial Landscape,” in Landscape and Power, ed. Mitchell, 14).

